{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 is incomplete (not merely limited): mutual recursion between argument attacks and warrant attacks is required to preserve the 'reinstatement as a derived property' guarantee in the expanded semantics. Suppose warrant W attacks argument X, and argument A attacks warrant W. In the inner pass (warrants only), W has no attackers and is accepted; in the outer pass (arguments only), A has no attackers and is accepted, so X is refuted by the already-accepted W. But A's successful attack on W should reinstate X by defeating its only attacker. Since the inner pass never sees A (an argument, evaluated only in the outer pass), W stays accepted and X stays refuted. Reinstatement of X therefore fails to be derived, violating the stated guarantee.","target":"07c3398912d45b4a9b8157672eff28042ba4869ae0a8c15bea92c79aa3532b64"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 violates the 'validity of every committed root' guarantee. A committed root R is supposed to be untouched by added edges. But if there exists a support relation B supports A together with an existing attack R→A, the construction adds a new edge B→R. If B is accepted in the grounded extension, this new edge defeats R, so the root is no longer unconditionally valid. The proposal lets an accepted supporter of A counterattack R, overturning a committed root via an edge the proposal introduces, which is precisely the guarantee the problem requires be preserved.","target":"7a13b68e008c1b3c8e76300f831ba5702fb8c530c4019ac1cba3635ed3f6aace"},{"attack":false,"case":"SRC_003 adds post-hoc depth tags derived from the already-computed grounded fixed point, so the fixpoint iteration itself is unchanged. Therefore determinism of the fixed point, polynomial cost, and reinstatement as a derived property are all preserved by construction. Committed roots get depth 0 and remain valid. The stated weakness (tags are explanatory only and cannot change acceptance) is a scope limitation, not a failure of any of the four required guarantees, and the problem asks to strengthen the semantics while preserving guarantees, which this does without violating them.","target":"d3d36f8fa4abaa28793092777dc7795fc2b7fd3ced65683496f9789c7c90b6c6"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 (contrast) aside, SRC_004's own weakness creates an ambiguity that breaks the 'determinism of the fixed point' guarantee. After expanding A→B into edge-node E→B, the proposal leaves undecided whether the original attack A→B still exists when E is out. If it remains, then B is still attacked by A even when its warrant is defeated; if it is removed, then B is reinstated. Both choices are consistent with the text, and they yield different grounded extensions for the same input. Thus the resulting fixed point is not uniquely determined by the specification, violating determinism.","target":"d98a41dc58d47192ce4409633e875f6ad8e76c989ea29fbde1a14c0926310741"}]}